/* Domain 8 — Ethical Hacking & Penetration Testing (methodology, ethics, standards, risk, reporting) */
BOOTCAMP.phases.push({
id:"p8", num:8, title:"Ethical Hacking & Penetration Testing", short:"Pentest",
tagline:"Finding weaknesses legally, on contract, so they get fixed — the profession, its method, its standards and its ethics.",
prereq:"Domains 2, 3, 5 and 7 are required. This domain is a professional-practice course for authorized security assessment; every skill is built in sanctioned labs and used only on systems you own or are contracted in writing to test.",
outcomes:[
"State the law and ethics that separate a penetration tester from a criminal, and operate strictly inside written scope and rules of engagement.",
"Explain the standard methodologies and frameworks — PTES, NIST SP 800-115, OWASP WSTG and ASVS — and run an engagement by them.",
"Threat-model a target and explain the major vulnerability classes by root cause, prevention, detection and how each finding is reported.",
"Score and prioritize findings with CVSS 4.0, EPSS and KEV against business context, not raw severity.",
"Assess web, cloud and Active Directory estates as configuration and design review, mapping every offensive concept to a defensive control.",
"Work with defenders: purple teaming, detection validation mapped to MITRE ATT&CK, evidence handling, report writing, remediation verification and retesting.",
"Map and prepare for the certification ladder: Security+ → eJPT/PenTest+ → OSCP+/GPEN, plus CEH, and know the current exam facts."
],
texts:[
{title:"Penetration Testing Execution Standard (PTES)", by:"PTES", note:"The seven-section methodology framework this domain follows: pre-engagement through reporting.", url:"https://www.pentest-standard.org/"},
{title:"NIST SP 800-115, Technical Guide to Information Security Testing and Assessment", by:"NIST", note:"The free US-government methodology: planning, discovery, attack, reporting, with a rules-of-engagement template.", url:"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/115/final"},
{title:"OWASP Web Security Testing Guide (WSTG) and Application Security Verification Standard (ASVS)", by:"OWASP", note:"The free, authoritative catalogue of what to test in web apps (WSTG) and the requirements to verify against (ASVS 5.0).", url:"https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/"},
{title:"CompTIA PenTest+ (PT0-003) and OffSec PEN-200 (OSCP+) objectives", by:"CompTIA / OffSec", note:"The certification blueprints: planning, scoping, legal, methodology and reporting weigh heavily on both.", url:"https://www.comptia.org/en-us/certifications/pentest/"}
],
modules:[
{id:"p8m1", title:"Ethics, Law and the Rules of Engagement", minutes:50,
outcomes:["State the one rule that defines ethical hacking.","Name the laws and documents that authorize your work.","Explain scope, rules of engagement, authorization and responsible disclosure.","Read a bug-bounty or safe-harbour policy as the contract it is.","Recognize when to stop and escalate."],
body:`<p>The question this module answers is the one every employer, client and background check will ask you to answer with your whole career: what makes you a professional rather than a criminal, when the actions are often identical? The answer is a single word — <strong>authorization</strong> — and the rest of the module is how a professional makes that word real on paper. This comes first because every later skill is built on it, and because the gap between "I had permission" and "I thought I had permission" is the gap between a paycheck and a felony.</p>
<h4>The one rule</h4>
<p>Never touch a system you do not own unless you hold explicit, written permission from someone with the authority to grant it, and you stay strictly inside the agreed <strong>scope</strong>. Not implied permission, not "they seemed fine with it", not a verbal dare from a recruiter — a signed document naming the targets, the dates, the allowed methods and the forbidden ones. The target's weakness never authorizes testing; only the owner does.</p>
<h4>The law (US, in brief, and not legal advice)</h4>
<p>The <strong>Computer Fraud and Abuse Act</strong> (CFAA, 18 U.S.C. &sect; 1030) makes it a federal crime to access a computer "without authorization" or to "exceed authorized access"; most states have their own statutes, and other countries have equivalents such as the UK Computer Misuse Act. Authorization is the entire defense. There is no "I was just curious", no "I didn't change anything", no "it was already insecure". In <strong>Van Buren v. United States</strong> (2021) the Supreme Court read "exceeds authorized access" narrowly: it covers reaching areas of a system that are off-limits to you — a gates-up-or-down question about whether you were entitled to be there at all — not merely using access you legitimately have for a disapproved purpose. That narrowing helps researchers at the margins, but it changes nothing about the core: reach into a system you were never permitted to enter and you are on the wrong side of the line.</p>
<p>In May 2022 the US Department of Justice revised its CFAA charging policy to say it will not charge <strong>good-faith security research</strong> — accessing a system solely to test, investigate or correct a security flaw, in a manner designed to avoid harm. Read that as prosecutorial discretion, not a licence: it does not bind state prosecutors, it does not touch civil liability, and "good faith" evaporates the moment you extort, exfiltrate or exceed what the research required. The safe position is unchanged — act only under written authorization.</p>
<h4>The documents that make you legitimate</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope</strong>: exactly which systems, applications, IP ranges and accounts are in-bounds, and which are explicitly out. Third parties — a cloud provider, a payment processor, a shared host — are usually out of scope and separately protected by their own rules.</li>
<li><strong>Rules of Engagement (RoE)</strong>: allowed techniques, test windows (often off-hours), rate limits, whether social engineering or denial-of-service is permitted (usually not), data-handling rules, stop conditions, and a named emergency contact reachable during the test. NIST SP 800-115 Appendix B is a public RoE template; learn its fields.</li>
<li><strong>Authorization letter</strong> (the "get-out-of-jail" letter): signed by someone who actually owns or controls the systems, naming you, the targets and the dates, carried for the duration of the test.</li>
<li><strong>NDA and a master services agreement</strong>: you will see secrets and you are legally bound to protect them; the MSA sets liability, insurance and indemnification.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Bug bounties and safe harbour</h4>
<p>A <strong>bug-bounty program</strong> is written authorization within a published scope — and only within it. The program page is the contract: it lists in-scope domains and assets, forbidden actions (no social engineering, no physical attacks, no automated scanning that degrades service), and a <strong>safe-harbour</strong> statement promising not to pursue legal action for good-faith testing that follows the rules. The disclose.io project standardizes this language so researchers can recognize it. Read every word before you begin, because anything the policy does not list as in-scope is out-of-scope, and out-of-scope is unauthorized. A real cautionary tale: in 2019 two Coalfire testers (Wynn and DeMercurio) were arrested at a county courthouse in Dallas County, Iowa, mid-engagement, even though they were hired by the Iowa State Court Administration — the county disputed who could authorize a break-in test of its building. The county later settled for $600,000, but the testers still spent a night in jail. The lesson is not that physical testing is reckless; it is that authorization must come from someone with real authority over the specific target, and that law-enforcement notification belongs in the RoE.</p>
<h4>Responsible disclosure</h4>
<p>When you find a flaw — in an engagement or a bounty — you report it privately to the owner and give reasonable time to fix it before any public mention; <strong>coordinated disclosure</strong> is the norm the industry runs on. You never sell it, post it, or use it beyond what proves the point. New regimes are tightening the timeline on the vendors who ship the software: under the EU Cyber Resilience Act, from 11 September 2026 manufacturers must report actively exploited vulnerabilities to ENISA within 24 hours (early warning) and 72 hours (notification), with the rest of the Act applying from December 2027. You are often the person who starts that clock, so your report has to be clear and timely.</p>
<h4>When to stop</h4>
<p>If you find evidence of a real ongoing breach by someone else; if you stumble onto data outside scope (medical records, another company's systems); if an action could crash a production system people depend on — you stop, document, and escalate to your named contact immediately. A professional's judgment about when <em>not</em> to proceed is worth more than any technique, and it is exactly what a client is paying for. This is the discipline the whole Domain 7 defensive foundation was building toward, and it is what every later domain — reverse engineering (Domain 11), wireless and hardware (Domain 10), the dark web (Domain 47) — assumes you already carry.</p>
<h4>On the exam</h4>
<p>Both CompTIA PenTest+ (PT0-003) and OffSec's PEN-200 open with planning, scoping and legality; PenTest+ puts 13% of the exam on "Engagement management" alone. Expect scenario questions that hand you a tempting out-of-scope target or a verbal "just check it" and reward the answer that insists on written authorization, defined scope and a named point of contact. The legal and ethical reasoning here is not a preamble to the technical work — on the exam and in the job, it <em>is</em> the work.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>The career case for ethics.</strong> Employers run background checks and ask behavioral questions about precisely this; a security clearance or a cyber-insurance policy can turn on it. The pentesters with the best reputations are the ones clients trust in their most sensitive systems — trust built on never crossing the line. Consider the recruiter who says "prove your skills, break into our site and show us." The amateur does it and has just committed a crime for a company that now has leverage over them. The professional replies: "Gladly — send me a scoping document naming the in-scope assets and dates, a signed authorization from someone who owns them, and your rules of engagement, and I'll start Monday." If a real company cannot produce those, that is the answer. Your clean record and your judgment are the most valuable professional assets you own, worth more than any single skill.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"If a system is insecure, testing it is fair game.\" Authorization, not the target's weakness, is what makes testing legal.","\"I caused no damage, so it's fine.\" The CFAA criminalizes unauthorized access itself, damage or not; Van Buren narrowed one clause but not this core.","\"Bug bounties mean anything goes.\" Only what the published scope allows; everything else is unauthorized, and safe harbour protects only in-scope, good-faith testing."],
problems:[
{q:"A recruiter says 'prove your skills — break into our site and show us.' What do you do?", a:"Get it in writing with scope, dates and an authorization signature from an owner first, or decline. A verbal dare is not authorization; a real company provides a scoped engagement."},
{q:"During a test you find customer records belonging to a different company on a shared host. Next step?", a:"Stop, do not access further, document what you saw and how, and escalate to your named contact immediately — it's out of scope and possibly a separate breach."},
{q:"Why is denial-of-service usually forbidden in the RoE?", a:"It can take down production systems real people depend on; the goal is to find weaknesses, not cause outages. It's allowed only if explicitly contracted, and cloud providers forbid even simulated DoS without a separate approval."},
{q:"What did Van Buren v. United States change about the CFAA, and what did it not change?", a:"It read 'exceeds authorized access' narrowly — reaching off-limits areas, not misusing access you legitimately hold. It did not legalize accessing systems you were never authorized to enter; authorization is still the whole defense."},
{q:"You want to test a company through its bug-bounty program. What governs what you may do?", a:"The program's published scope and rules — in-scope assets, forbidden actions, and the safe-harbour statement. Anything not listed as in-scope is out-of-scope and unauthorized."},
{q:"Name the four documents that define a legitimate engagement.", a:"Scope, Rules of Engagement, a signed authorization letter from a system owner, and an NDA (usually under a master services agreement)."},
{q:"The Coalfire testers were hired by the state court administration but arrested by the county. What engagement-planning lesson does that teach?", a:"Authorization must come from someone with real authority over the specific target, and law-enforcement notification and a reachable emergency contact belong in the rules of engagement."}
],
checks:[{q:"What single thing makes hacking ethical and legal?",a:"Explicit written authorization within an agreed scope."},{q:"What is the RoE?",a:"Rules of Engagement: agreed techniques, timing, limits, data handling, stop conditions and emergency contact."},{q:"What is safe harbour in a bounty program?",a:"A written promise not to pursue legal action for good-faith testing that stays within the published scope."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA, in your own words, why you'd turn down a paid job to 'quietly check if a competitor's site is hackable,' citing the CFAA and scope; have ADA play the tempting client and push back."},

{id:"p8m2", title:"Methodology and Standards: PTES, NIST SP 800-115, OWASP and the Engagement", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Explain why a repeatable methodology matters and name the industry standards.","Walk the PTES seven sections and the NIST SP 800-115 four stages.","Run pre-engagement scoping and threat modelling.","Explain how each offensive phase maps to a defensive control and produces a client artifact."],
body:`<p>Professional penetration testing is not ad-hoc poking; it follows a repeatable <strong>methodology</strong> so results are thorough, consistent, defensible and safe. This module is the shape of a real engagement, and it is heavily weighted on every exam because the method — not any single trick — is what a client buys. You will anchor to published standards so that two testers, or you and the next tester a year later, cover the same ground.</p>
<h4>The standards you work from</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>PTES</strong> (Penetration Testing Execution Standard) defines seven sections: pre-engagement interactions, intelligence gathering, threat modelling, vulnerability analysis, exploitation, post-exploitation, and reporting. It is the common mental model of an engagement.</li>
<li><strong>NIST SP 800-115</strong>, the free US-government guide, frames a four-stage technical methodology — <em>planning, discovery, attack, reporting</em> — with the attack stage looping back to discovery as you learn more. It groups techniques into review, target identification and analysis, and target vulnerability validation, and its Appendix B is a rules-of-engagement template.</li>
<li><strong>OWASP</strong> gives you the web-specific depth: the <strong>Web Security Testing Guide</strong> (WSTG, stable v4.2 with v5 in development) is a checklist of what to test, with stable identifiers such as WSTG-ATHN for authentication; the <strong>Application Security Verification Standard</strong> (ASVS 5.0, May 2025) is the list of requirements you verify against, at levels L1 (basic), L2 (standard, most apps) and L3 (high-assurance).</li>
<li><strong>MITRE ATT&CK</strong> is the shared language of adversary tactics and techniques that lets you describe findings and defenders map detections (Module 8).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Pre-engagement: scoping and goals</h4>
<p>Before any testing, you and the client agree what and why. Is the goal to test a specific web app, the external network, the internal network (assuming a breached foothold), wireless, cloud, physical, or people (social engineering)? Is it <strong>black box</strong> (you know nothing, simulating an outsider), <strong>white box</strong> (full information, efficient and thorough), or <strong>grey box</strong> (partial, like a logged-in user)? You set the scope, rules of engagement, timing and success criteria (Module 1), and you write an <strong>assessment plan</strong>: objectives, scope, team roles, limitations, assumptions, timeline and deliverables. Good scoping is where most of a test's value is decided; under- or over-scoping both waste the client's money, and a missing third-party authorization can stop the test cold.</p>
<h4>Threat modelling</h4>
<p><strong>Threat modelling</strong> turns scope into a plan of attack-on-paper. You ask four questions: what are we building or protecting (the data and functions that matter); what can go wrong (which adversaries, with what goals — a method such as STRIDE from Domain 7 helps enumerate spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege); what are we going to do about it (which tests actually exercise those risks); and did we do a good enough job. Threat modelling is why a professional spends the client's limited hours on the login flow that guards every account rather than the marketing site that holds nothing — it directs effort to where impact lives. It connects directly to the business-risk reasoning you will use to prioritize findings in Module 6.</p>
<h4>The phases, each with its defensive mirror</h4>
<p>Every framework maps to the same lifecycle. For each, note what it <em>produces</em> for the client and the <em>defense</em> that counters it, because a pentester thinks in both directions:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Reconnaissance / intelligence gathering</strong>: map the public attack surface (Module 3). Produces an inventory of exposed assets. Defensive counter: minimize public exposure, attack-surface management (Domain 7).</li>
<li><strong>Scanning and enumeration</strong>: identify live systems, open services and versions, and known weaknesses. Produces a list of potential entry points. Defense: patching, hardening, closing unneeded services (Domain 7).</li>
<li><strong>Vulnerability analysis and gaining access</strong>: confirm that a weakness is real by safely proving it, to the minimum extent needed to show impact. Produces evidence a finding is exploitable, not destruction. Defense: secure coding, input validation, patching (Domains 5, 7).</li>
<li><strong>Post-exploitation</strong>: assess what the access actually means — what data or systems it reaches, how far an attacker could pivot — without causing harm. Produces the business impact. Defense: least privilege, segmentation, monitoring (Domains 7, 16).</li>
<li><strong>Reporting</strong>: document findings, risk and fixes. Produces the deliverable the client actually paid for (Module 4).</li>
</ol>
<p>Notice every offensive phase has a defensive mirror from Domain 7 — this is why the defensive domain comes first, and why the best pentesters are excellent defenders. The point of the exercise is never the break-in; it is a prioritized list of real weaknesses the client can fix before a real attacker finds them.</p>
<h4>Minimal impact and professionalism</h4>
<p>Throughout, a professional works with <strong>minimal impact</strong>: prove the finding, do not exploit it further than necessary, never destroy data, stay in scope, and keep careful notes and evidence (timestamps, screenshots, request/response pairs) for the report and for your own legal protection. If something could disrupt production you pause and confirm with your contact. The engagement is a controlled, documented, reversible assessment — closer to a financial audit than a heist — and that discipline is what makes it a trusted profession.</p>
<h4>On the exam</h4>
<p>PenTest+ (PT0-003) devotes 21% to reconnaissance and enumeration and 17% to vulnerability discovery and analysis; its domains map almost one-to-one onto PTES. Expect to order the phases, pick black/white/grey box for a stated goal, choose what belongs in a scope or RoE document, and recognize a methodology step from its deliverable. Knowing which standard owns which artifact — NIST's RoE template, OWASP's WSTG checklist, PTES's seven sections — is itself tested.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Offense and defense are one skill.</strong> A tester finds an outdated, internet-exposed service during enumeration. Offensively, that is a potential entry point to verify. Defensively, it is a patch-management and attack-surface failure (Domain 7). The report will not say "we broke in"; it will say "this service is outdated and internet-exposed, here is the evidence it is exploitable, here is the business impact if an attacker used it, and here is the remediation: patch it, restrict it, monitor it." Now add the methodology: threat modelling flagged that service as guarding customer data, so you spent your hours there rather than on the brochure site; PTES told you to confirm business impact in post-exploitation before writing it up; NIST's four stages kept you looping discovery and attack as you learned the environment; and the RoE told you to pause and call your contact before touching the one box the client flagged as fragile. The whole value is turning an offensive finding into a defensive fix, by a method anyone can audit.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Pentesting is creatively breaking in however you like.\" It's a scoped, methodical, documented process (PTES, NIST SP 800-115) with a defined goal and minimal impact.","\"The exciting phase is exploitation.\" Scoping, threat modelling and reporting carry most of the professional value; exploitation just proves a finding is real.","\"A pentester only thinks offensively.\" Every phase mirrors a Domain 7 defense; the output is fixes, so defensive mastery is required."],
problems:[
{q:"Name the seven PTES sections in order.", a:"Pre-engagement interactions, intelligence gathering, threat modelling, vulnerability analysis, exploitation, post-exploitation, reporting."},
{q:"How do the NIST SP 800-115 four stages relate to the five-phase model, and what is distinctive about them?", a:"Planning, discovery, attack, reporting — the attack stage loops back to discovery as you learn more; it collapses recon/scan into discovery and treats the loop explicitly."},
{q:"Contrast black-box, white-box and grey-box testing and say when each is appropriate.", a:"Black box: no prior knowledge, simulates an outsider, realistic but slow. White box: full information, most thorough and efficient, best for depth. Grey box: partial, like an authenticated user, a common balance."},
{q:"What does threat modelling add before you touch a tool?", a:"It identifies what matters (data/functions), what can go wrong (adversaries and STRIDE categories), and which tests exercise those risks — directing limited hours to where impact lives."},
{q:"Give the defensive counter (from Domain 7) for the scanning/enumeration phase.", a:"Patching, hardening, and closing unneeded services to shrink the attack surface, plus attack-surface management."},
{q:"What belongs in an assessment plan under NIST SP 800-115?", a:"Objectives, scope, team roles, limitations, assumptions, resources, timeline and deliverables — run the engagement like a project."},
{q:"Which OWASP document do you verify requirements against, and what are its levels?", a:"ASVS 5.0; levels L1 (basic), L2 (standard, most applications) and L3 (high-assurance)."}
],
checks:[{q:"What is PTES?",a:"The Penetration Testing Execution Standard — a seven-section methodology framework."},{q:"The four NIST SP 800-115 stages?",a:"Planning, discovery, attack, reporting."},{q:"What does every offensive phase have?",a:"A defensive counterpart from Domain 7."}],
explain:"Walk ADA through an engagement using PTES and the NIST four stages, and for each phase name the Domain 7 defense that counters it and the artifact the client receives."},

{id:"p8m3", title:"The Toolkit and Where to Build the Skill — Legally", minutes:45,
outcomes:["Name the standard categories of assessment tooling and what each is for.","Explain the role of a testing distribution and an isolated home lab.","List the sanctioned platforms where skills are built legally.","Explain why fundamentals underlie every tool and how a professional reads tool output."],
body:`<p>Pentesters use a well-known set of tools, but tools are the easy part — they are only as good as the fundamentals (Domains 2, 3, 5, 7) behind them. This module maps the toolkit at a high level, explains <em>why</em> each category exists, and, more importantly, shows you the legal places to build real skill. We stay conceptual: the value is in reading what a tool tells you, not in command recipes.</p>
<h4>The tool categories</h4>
<p>You do not need command incantations here; you need to know what exists, what problem it solves, and what its output means. The standard categories:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A testing distribution</strong>: <strong>Kali Linux</strong> (and Parrot OS) package the common tools on a ready-to-use Linux (Domain 3), run in a VM. It saves setup time; it is not magic.</li>
<li><strong>Reconnaissance / OSINT tools</strong>: gather public information about an organization's attack surface — domains, certificates, exposed hosts — so you know what is exposed before you probe. Domain 32 covers OSINT in depth.</li>
<li><strong>Scanners</strong>: network and port scanners (the famous one is <strong>nmap</strong>) map live hosts and services; vulnerability scanners (Nessus, OpenVAS/Greenbone) match versions and configurations against known-weakness databases. These are the same tools defenders run to audit themselves.</li>
<li><strong>Web proxies</strong>: <strong>Burp Suite</strong> (and OWASP ZAP) sit between browser and server so you can inspect and understand the HTTP requests an application trusts — the core of web application review (Domains 5, 31).</li>
<li><strong>Exploitation frameworks</strong>: <strong>Metasploit</strong> organizes published, known vulnerabilities so a finding can be safely verified in an authorized test. Note that OSCP+ restricts it to one target and bans it for pivoting — exams test method, not tool reliance.</li>
<li><strong>Credential-audit, cloud-assessment and reporting tools</strong> round out the kit; the cloud and directory tools (Module 7) are configuration reviewers more than anything else.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every one of these is <strong>dual-use</strong>: defenders run the same scanners and proxies to find their own weaknesses first. That is the honest framing — these are security tools, and authorization is what makes their use legitimate (Module 1).</p>
<h4>Your legal practice ground</h4>
<p>You never practice on the live internet. You build skill in environments made and licensed for it:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>TryHackMe</strong> and <strong>Hack The Box</strong>: guided rooms and vulnerable machines in legal sandboxes, with learning paths from beginner to advanced.</li>
<li><strong>PortSwigger Web Security Academy</strong>: free, excellent, hands-on web-vulnerability labs in the vendor's own sandbox, mapped to the WSTG.</li>
<li><strong>Deliberately vulnerable apps you run in your own VM</strong>: OWASP Juice Shop, DVWA, Metasploitable and VulnHub images, isolated on a host-only network so nothing reaches the real internet (your Domain 10 / Domain 30 home-lab build).</li>
<li><strong>CTFs</strong> (capture the flag) and <strong>picoCTF</strong>: competitions that teach by legal challenge.</li>
</ul>
<p>These give you unlimited, lawful practice. The discipline of only ever testing these, plus systems you are contracted to test, is the habit that defines the profession — and the one OSCP's proctored, rules-bound exam is built to prove you have.</p>
<h4>Choosing the right tool for the estate</h4>
<p>Part of professional judgment is matching the tool category to the target, because the wrong tool wastes the client's hours. A network scanner answers "what is reachable and what is it running"; a vulnerability scanner answers "which known weaknesses might those versions have" and always over-reports, so its output is a list of <em>candidates</em> you must verify. A web proxy is the tool for anything that speaks HTTP, because the questions that matter in a web app — is authorization enforced server-side, is input treated as data — are visible only in the requests the application trusts. Cloud and directory reviews (Module 7) lean on read-only configuration readers more than on anything that "attacks." The mistake a beginner makes is reaching for an exploitation framework first; the professional reaches for it last, only to safely confirm a specific candidate already understood. This is the same sequencing the methodology enforces in Module 2: understand before you verify, verify with minimal impact, and never run a tool whose output you could not explain in the report.</p>
<h4>Fundamentals first — and how a pro reads output</h4>
<p>A tool that scans or proxies is useless if you do not understand what it shows you, which is why Domains 2 (networking), 3 (Linux), 5 (web) and 7 (security) come first. A beginner runs a scanner and drowns in a 400-line report; a professional reads it because they understand TCP, services and the application, and can tell a real, reachable, serious finding from a false positive or an irrelevant one. That reading is the skill — the tool only surfaces candidates. The same truth runs through the whole curriculum: Domain 9's lesson about AI tools, Domain 11's about disassemblers, Domain 28's about network automation — the tool accelerates an expert and overwhelms a novice. Everything you verify, you verify so you can explain it in the report (Module 4); a finding you cannot explain is a finding you cannot defend.</p>
<h4>On the exam</h4>
<p>PenTest+ domain "Tools and code analysis" and the recon/enumeration domains expect you to match a tool category to a task and to interpret sample output — read a scan result and say what it means and what you would verify next, not type a command from memory. OSCP's tool restrictions (Metasploit limited to one target, no automated exploitation, no AI assistants during the exam) exist precisely to test whether you understand the fundamentals under the tools. Study the categories and what their output means; the specific flags you can always look up.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Why the fundamentals decide everything.</strong> Two people run the same vulnerability scanner against the same authorized target and get the same 200-line report. The one who did Domains 2, 3 and 7 recognizes which findings are real and serious, which are false positives from a backported-but-unversioned patch, and what each means for the business — and writes a report the client can act on. The one who just learned the tool forwards the raw output, which is worthless and often wrong: it flags a 'critical' on an internal host that no attacker can reach and misses the medium-rated authentication gap on the internet-facing login that actually matters. The scanner is identical; the fundamentals are the whole difference, and they are what this curriculum is really building.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Learning the tools is learning pentesting.\" Tools surface candidates; understanding networks, systems and apps (Domains 2/3/5/7) is what makes the data meaningful and the report correct.","\"These are hacker tools.\" They're dual-use security tools defenders use too; authorization, not the tool, determines legitimacy.","\"I can practice on real sites to learn.\" Never; use TryHackMe/HTB/PortSwigger and your own isolated VMs — the only legal practice."],
problems:[
{q:"What is Kali Linux, and where do you run it?", a:"A Linux distribution packaging common security-testing tools; you run it in a VM in an isolated lab. It saves setup time, not thinking."},
{q:"Name two sanctioned platforms for legally building pentest skills.", a:"Any two: TryHackMe, Hack The Box, PortSwigger Web Security Academy, picoCTF, or your own VMs with OWASP Juice Shop/DVWA/VulnHub."},
{q:"Why are a port scanner and a web proxy considered dual-use?", a:"Defenders run the same tools to audit and find their own weaknesses first; authorization is what distinguishes legitimate use."},
{q:"Why do Domains 2, 3, 5 and 7 come before the tooling?", a:"The tools only surface candidates; you need networking, Linux, web and security knowledge to interpret output, reject false positives and act on it."},
{q:"What makes a home lab safe and legal to attack?", a:"It's your own vulnerable VMs on an isolated/host-only network, so nothing touches systems you don't own or the internet."},
{q:"Two testers get the same 200-line scan report and produce reports of very different value. Why?", a:"Fundamentals: one can tell reachable, serious findings from false positives and judge business impact; the other just forwards raw output."},
{q:"Why does OSCP restrict Metasploit and ban AI assistants during the exam?", a:"To test the fundamentals under the tools — that you can enumerate, analyze and verify by understanding, not by running an automated or outsourced shortcut."}
],
checks:[{q:"The famous network scanner?",a:"nmap."},{q:"The core web-testing proxy?",a:"Burp Suite (or OWASP ZAP)."},{q:"Where do you practice legally?",a:"TryHackMe/HTB/PortSwigger and your own isolated VMs."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA why two people with the same tools and the same scan output produce reports of completely different value, and why the difference is Domains 2, 3, 5 and 7, not the tool."},

{id:"p8m4", title:"The Deliverable: Reporting, Remediation, Retesting and the Profession", minutes:50,
outcomes:["Explain why the report is the real product of a pentest.","Structure a professional finding with risk rating and remediation.","Explain evidence handling, remediation verification and retesting.","Map the certification ladder with current exam facts and how to enter the field."],
body:`<p>Clients do not pay to be hacked; they pay for a clear, actionable understanding of their weaknesses and how to fix them. The <strong>report</strong> is the product. A brilliant break-in with a poor report is a failed engagement; a modest finding with an excellent report is a success. This module — and the reporting-and-communication weighting on every exam — treats reporting, remediation and retesting as the core of the craft, not an afterthought.</p>
<h4>Evidence handling first</h4>
<p>Throughout the engagement you keep an evidence trail: timestamped notes, screenshots, and the request/response pairs that prove each finding, stored securely because they contain the client's secrets. Good evidence is what makes a finding <em>reproducible</em> — an engineer can follow your steps and see the same result — and it protects you legally by showing you stayed in scope and caused no harm. Each candidate is validated to rule out a <strong>false positive</strong> before it becomes a finding, because a report full of unconfirmed alerts destroys the client's trust. Redact credentials and personal data in the report itself; keep the raw evidence encrypted and delete it per the contract's retention terms when the engagement closes.</p>
<h4>Anatomy of a report</h4>
<p>A professional report has two audiences and two halves. The <strong>executive summary</strong> (for leadership): the overall risk posture in plain language, the most important findings, and business impact, with no jargon. The <strong>technical findings</strong> (for the engineers who will fix them): each finding written as a self-contained unit with:</p>
<ul>
<li>A clear title and description of the weakness and its root cause.</li>
<li>A <strong>risk rating</strong> — severity via <strong>CVSS</strong> (Module 6), tempered by likelihood and business context into a real risk level — so the client knows what to fix first.</li>
<li>Reproducible evidence (screenshots, request/response, steps) proving it is real.</li>
<li>Business impact: what an attacker could actually achieve, in the client's terms.</li>
<li><strong>Remediation</strong>: concrete, prioritized guidance to fix it — and, where possible, the root-cause fix that closes a whole class, not just the instance (the most valuable part, where Domain 7 knowledge pays off).</li>
</ul>
<p>Findings are prioritized by business risk so the client spends effort where it matters. The report is written to be read and acted on; clarity is a core professional skill, which is why the curriculum drilled explaining and teaching throughout.</p>
<h4>Remediation verification and retest</h4>
<p>After the client fixes the findings, a good engagement includes a <strong>retest</strong> to verify the fixes actually worked — not that a ticket was closed, but that the weakness is genuinely gone and no new one was introduced by the fix. You re-run the specific checks that produced each finding and update its status to verified-fixed, partially-fixed or still-open. Closing this loop is what turns a report into a safer system. You present the findings, answer questions, and often help the team understand the root causes so they prevent whole classes of issues. The relationship — trusted advisor, not adversary — is what earns repeat work, and retesting is often where it is won.</p>
<h4>Writing for two readers at once</h4>
<p>The hardest craft in the report is serving leadership and engineers in one document without boring or losing either. Leadership needs the <em>so what</em>: how exposed is the business today, which two or three things matter this quarter, and what it costs to ignore them — in money and risk, not jargon. Engineers need the <em>exactly how</em>: the affected asset, the reproducible steps, the root cause, and a fix specific enough to implement without guessing. A good report keeps these in separate sections so neither reader has to wade through the other's, and it uses the same risk ordering in both so the executive summary's "fix these first" matches the technical section's priority ranking. Clarity here is not decoration; a finding an engineer cannot reproduce is a finding that will be closed as "cannot reproduce," and a risk a manager cannot understand is a risk that will not be funded. This is why the whole curriculum drilled teaching-back and plain explanation — the report is where it pays the client's bill.</p>
<h4>The certification ladder (verified October 2026)</h4>
<p>Build credentials in order, each with hands-on practice:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>CompTIA Security+</strong> (Domain 7): the defensive foundation employers expect first.</li>
<li><strong>eJPT</strong> (INE Security) or <strong>CompTIA PenTest+</strong> (PT0-003): entry-level offensive certs covering methodology, scoping, legal and reporting plus practical skill. The eJPT is a 48-hour, hands-on, open-ended practical (45 questions as of the 2026 update) and is beginner-friendly; PenTest+ (PT0-003, launched December 2024) is up to 90 multiple-choice and performance-based questions in 165 minutes, 750/900 to pass, and is DoD 8140-approved.</li>
<li><strong>CEH</strong> (EC-Council, v13): widely requested by HR filters; 125 questions in 4 hours; broad but less hands-on than the practical exams.</li>
<li><strong>OSCP+</strong> (OffSec PEN-200): the respected, ~24-hour proctored practical exam that proves you can actually do the work — an assumed-breach Active Directory set worth 40 points plus standalone targets, 70/100 to pass, a professional report required within 24 hours afterward. The "+" (introduced late 2024) is valid three years and renewed by CPE or a higher OffSec exam; the original OSCP remains a lifetime credential. This is the milestone that opens pentest roles.</li>
<li><strong>GIAC GPEN</strong> (SANS SEC560): a performance-based alternative respected in enterprise and government.</li>
</ol>
<p>Beyond entry level: OSWE/Burp Suite Certified Practitioner for web, OSEP for advanced, and specialties in cloud, mobile and hardware. See the certification track card for the fully verified facts.</p>
<h4>Breaking in</h4>
<p>Most pentesters start in defense — a SOC or sysadmin role (Domains 3, 7, 21) — build fundamentals, practice relentlessly on TryHackMe/HTB, earn Security+ then a pentest cert, document their learning (a blog, a home lab, CTF write-ups), and move across. The field rewards demonstrable skill and trustworthiness over credentials alone: a strong practice record and a clean ethical reputation (Module 1) are what get you hired. The work is a genuine craft — methodical, creative within rules, and deeply tied to helping organizations defend themselves.</p>
<h4>On the exam</h4>
<p>PenTest+ and PEN-200 both make the written report a scored deliverable; OSCP fails candidates who break in but document poorly. Expect questions that hand you findings and ask you to order them by business risk, to identify what a good finding must contain, or to explain why a retest matters. The skill the exams reward is exactly the job: turning raw results into a prioritized, verifiable, readable plan the client will act on.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Two findings, same severity, prioritized by the report.</strong> A test turns up a high-severity flaw on an internal printer and a medium flaw on the public customer-login page. A naive list ranks the printer higher by raw CVSS. A professional report weighs business impact and exposure: the internet-facing login handling customer data is the one to fix first despite the lower raw score, because the risk (likelihood &times; impact, informed by EPSS and exposure — Module 6) is greater. The finding is written with its root cause, reproducible evidence, the business impact in the client's own terms, and a remediation that fixes the class. Three weeks later the retest confirms the login fix holds and no new issue was introduced, and the printer finding is downgraded with a segmentation note. That judgment — turning raw findings into a prioritized, verified remediation plan — is exactly what the client is paying a professional for, and what separates a report that gets acted on from one that gets ignored.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"The hack is the deliverable.\" The report is; clarity, risk-prioritization, remediation guidance and a retest are the product clients pay for.","\"List findings by raw severity.\" Prioritize by business risk (likelihood × impact and exposure), not raw CVSS alone.","\"OSCP first.\" Build Security+ and fundamentals first; OSCP+ is a later, hands-on milestone, not a starting point."],
problems:[
{q:"Why is the report considered the real product of a penetration test?", a:"Clients pay to understand and fix their weaknesses; a clear, prioritized, actionable report plus a retest is what enables that — the break-in only proves findings are real."},
{q:"Name the elements of a well-written technical finding.", a:"Title/description with root cause, risk rating (CVSS tempered by business context), reproducible evidence, business impact, and concrete prioritized remediation."},
{q:"What is remediation verification, and how is a retest more than closing a ticket?", a:"Re-running the specific checks that produced each finding to confirm the weakness is genuinely gone and no new one was introduced — a closed ticket is not proof the fix worked."},
{q:"How should evidence be handled during and after an engagement?", a:"Keep timestamped, reproducible evidence stored securely; redact secrets and personal data in the report; keep raw evidence encrypted and delete it per the contract's retention terms at close."},
{q:"Put the cert ladder in order with one current fact each: OSCP+, Security+, PenTest+/eJPT, CEH.", a:"Security+ (foundation) → eJPT (48-hour practical) / PenTest+ PT0-003 (up to 90 Q, 165 min, 750/900) → CEH v13 (125 Q, 4 h, HR breadth) → OSCP+ (~24-hour assumed-breach practical, 70/100, report required)."},
{q:"How do most people actually break into pentesting?", a:"Start in defense (SOC/sysadmin), build fundamentals, practice on TryHackMe/HTB, earn Security+ then a pentest cert, document their work, and move across."},
{q:"Why does the defensive domain (Domain 7) come before this one?", a:"Every offensive phase mirrors a defensive control; you can't write useful remediation or avoid harm without defensive mastery."}
],
checks:[{q:"The real product of a pentest?",a:"The report (clear, prioritized findings + remediation) plus a retest."},{q:"How are findings ordered?",a:"By business risk (likelihood × impact/exposure), most important first."},{q:"The respected hands-on pentest exam?",a:"OSCP+ (OffSec PEN-200)."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA why a modest finding with an excellent, actionable report and a clean retest beats a dramatic break-in with a poor one, from the client's point of view."},

{id:"p8m5", title:"Threat Modelling and Vulnerability Classes: Root Causes, Prevention and Detection", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Explain the major web and system vulnerability classes by root cause, not recipe.","Name the prevention and detection for each class and how the finding is reported.","Use the OWASP Top 10:2025, WSTG and ASVS to structure coverage.","Explain why one root cause (untrusted input crossing a trust boundary) underlies most classes."],
body:`<p>The question this module answers: when a report lists "SQL injection" or "broken access control," what is actually wrong, why does it happen, how is it prevented, and how would a defender notice it? A professional assessor explains the <em>mechanism</em> and the <em>fix</em>, never the attack recipe. That is also the honest division of labour in this curriculum — the operational practice happens only in the sanctioned labs of Module 3, under authorization; here we build the understanding that lets you recognize, prevent, detect and report each class.</p>
<h4>The one root cause behind most of them</h4>
<p>Most application vulnerabilities reduce to a single pattern: <strong>untrusted input crossing a trust boundary</strong> and being treated as something more privileged than data — as code, as a command, as a filename, as an identity. The <strong>trust boundary</strong> is the line between what the attacker controls (the request) and what the system trusts (its database, its filesystem, its session). Keep input strictly as data on the trusted side and whole classes vanish. This is why secure design (Domain 5, Domain 7) prevents far more than any filter.</p>
<h4>The OWASP Top 10:2025 as a map</h4>
<p>The <strong>OWASP Top 10</strong> is the industry's shared list of the most critical web application risks; the 2025 edition is: A01 Broken Access Control, A02 Security Misconfiguration, A03 Software Supply Chain Failures, A04 Cryptographic Failures, A05 Injection, A06 Insecure Design, A07 Authentication Failures, A08 Software or Data Integrity Failures, A09 Security Logging and Alerting Failures, A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions. Use it to structure coverage; use the <strong>WSTG</strong> for the concrete checks under each, and <strong>ASVS</strong> for the requirement you verify against.</p>
<h4>The major classes, by root cause / prevention / detection / reporting</h4>
<table><tr><th>Class</th><th>Root cause</th><th>Prevention</th><th>Detection</th></tr>
<tr><td>Injection (SQL, OS, LDAP)</td><td>Input mixed into a command or query as code</td><td>Parameterized queries, safe APIs, strict input validation, least-privilege DB accounts</td><td>WAF and DB logs, anomalous query errors, input-validation alerts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broken access control</td><td>The server trusts the client to enforce "who may do what"</td><td>Enforce authorization server-side on every request; deny by default</td><td>Access logs showing one user reaching another's objects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication failures</td><td>Weak proof of identity; guessable or reused secrets</td><td>MFA, strong password policy, rate limiting, secure session handling (Domain 7 IAM)</td><td>Spikes in failed logins, impossible-travel sign-ins</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cross-site scripting (XSS)</td><td>Untrusted input rendered as active content in a page</td><td>Context-aware output encoding, Content-Security-Policy, framework auto-escaping</td><td>CSP violation reports, unexpected script in logs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Server-side request forgery (SSRF)</td><td>Server fetches a URL the attacker controls</td><td>Allow-list outbound destinations, block internal ranges and metadata endpoints</td><td>Outbound requests to internal/metadata addresses in logs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insecure deserialization / integrity</td><td>Trusting serialized data or unsigned updates</td><td>Signed, verified data and updates; avoid native deserialization of untrusted input</td><td>Integrity-check failures, unexpected object types</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security misconfiguration</td><td>Defaults, verbose errors, open storage, missing hardening</td><td>Hardened baselines, least privilege, config as code, remove defaults</td><td>Config drift alerts, exposed admin panels in scans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Supply-chain failures</td><td>A trusted dependency or build pipeline is compromised</td><td>Pin and verify dependencies, SBOMs, signed artifacts, review third-party code</td><td>Dependency-scanning alerts, unexpected build changes</td></tr></table>
<h4>How each becomes a finding</h4>
<p>In the report, a vulnerability-class finding is never "I ran X and got a shell." It is: the root cause in one sentence (for injection, "the application builds a database query by concatenating user input, so input can change the query's meaning"), reproducible evidence that it is real and reachable, the business impact in the client's terms (what data or function is exposed), and the remediation that closes the class (parameterized queries and least-privilege DB access), with the WSTG reference and the ASVS requirement it violates. Written that way, a finding teaches the engineer to prevent the whole class, which is the highest-value thing a report can do.</p>
<h4>Beyond the web</h4>
<p>The same thinking applies system-wide: memory-safety flaws (Domain 15) are untrusted input crossing the boundary of a buffer; command injection is input crossing into a shell; directory traversal is input crossing into the filesystem. Reverse engineering (Domain 11) studies these at the binary level — again, defensively and on your own code. Across all of them the professional's contribution is the same: name the class, explain the root cause, prove it safely, and hand over a fix and a detection.</p>
<h4>Why defense-in-depth still matters per class</h4>
<p>Naming one root cause does not mean one control is enough. Each class has a <em>primary</em> fix at the source — parameterized queries for injection, server-side authorization for access control, output encoding for XSS — and secondary, compensating controls that reduce impact when the primary fix is missed: least-privilege database accounts so an injection reaches less, a Content-Security-Policy so an XSS does less, network egress rules so an SSRF reaches fewer internal targets. A professional reports the primary fix first, because it closes the class, and lists the compensating controls as defence-in-depth, because real systems ship with mistakes and the layers buy time for detection and patching. This mirrors the Domain 7 principle exactly: no single control is trusted to be perfect, so the design assumes one will fail and ensures another is watching.</p>
<h4>On the exam</h4>
<p>PenTest+ (vulnerability discovery and analysis, 17%) and CEH both test recognition: given a symptom, name the class and the best mitigation. OSCP expects you to understand classes deeply enough to exploit them under authorization in the lab — but even there the deliverable is a report that explains root cause and remediation. ASVS and the OWASP Top 10 are the frameworks the exams assume you know; learn the classes by cause and fix, and the recall takes care of itself.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>One root cause, four reports.</strong> On an authorized web assessment you find four issues that all trace to "untrusted input crossing a trust boundary." A search box concatenates input into a database query (injection, A05) — remediation: parameterized queries. A profile page shows other users' records by changing an ID in the request (broken access control, A01) — remediation: server-side authorization on every object. A comment field renders raw HTML (XSS) — remediation: context-aware output encoding plus a Content-Security-Policy. An image-import feature fetches any URL you give it, including internal addresses (SSRF, under A06 insecure design) — remediation: an outbound allow-list that blocks internal and metadata ranges. You did not need four tricks; you needed one principle. The report groups them under that principle, so the engineering team fixes the design habit, not just the four instances — and you add the detection for each (DB error monitoring, access-log review, CSP violation reports, outbound-request alerts) so the blue team can catch the next one.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Each vulnerability is its own unrelated trick.\" Most reduce to untrusted input crossing a trust boundary and being treated as code/command/identity; one principle prevents many.","\"A WAF fixes injection and XSS.\" A WAF is a detective/compensating control; the fix is parameterized queries and output encoding at the source.","\"Finding the bug is the job.\" Explaining its root cause, prevention, detection and remediation in the report is the job."],
problems:[
{q:"What single root cause underlies injection, XSS, SSRF and path traversal?", a:"Untrusted input crossing a trust boundary and being treated as something privileged — code, a command, a URL, a filename — instead of inert data."},
{q:"How do parameterized queries prevent SQL injection at the root?", a:"They send user input strictly as data, bound to placeholders, so it can never change the query's structure or meaning."},
{q:"A profile page shows other users' data by changing an ID in the URL. Name the class and the fix.", a:"Broken access control (A01); enforce authorization server-side on every object request and deny by default — never trust the client to hide it."},
{q:"Give the prevention and the detection for cross-site scripting.", a:"Prevention: context-aware output encoding and a Content-Security-Policy (plus framework auto-escaping). Detection: CSP violation reports and unexpected script in logs."},
{q:"Why is a WAF not a remediation for injection?", a:"It is a compensating/detective control that can be bypassed; the remediation is fixing the code to treat input as data (parameterized queries, least-privilege DB accounts)."},
{q:"How should a vulnerability-class finding be written in the report?", a:"Root cause in one sentence, reproducible evidence that it's real and reachable, business impact in the client's terms, class-closing remediation, and the WSTG/ASVS reference."},
{q:"What are the OWASP Top 10, WSTG and ASVS each for?", a:"Top 10: the shared list of top web risks (map). WSTG: the concrete checks to perform. ASVS: the requirements you verify against, by level."}
],
checks:[{q:"The common root cause of most app flaws?",a:"Untrusted input crossing a trust boundary, treated as privileged."},{q:"A01 in the OWASP Top 10:2025?",a:"Broken Access Control."},{q:"What does ASVS give you?",a:"Verifiable security requirements at levels L1–L3."}],
explain:"Pick three vulnerability classes and explain each to ADA by root cause, prevention, detection and how you'd word the finding — without any attack steps."},

{id:"p8m6", title:"Risk and Severity: CVSS 4.0, EPSS, KEV and Business Context", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Distinguish severity from risk and explain why CVSS alone is not a priority.","Read and reason about a CVSS 4.0 vector and its metric groups.","Combine CVSS with EPSS, KEV and business context to prioritize.","Turn a queue of findings into a defensible remediation order with worked arithmetic."],
body:`<p>The question this module answers: of the dozens of findings a test produces, which does the client fix first, and how do you defend that order when their budget only covers half? The answer is the difference between <strong>severity</strong> (how bad the flaw is in the abstract) and <strong>risk</strong> (how likely it is to hurt <em>this</em> business and how much). Getting this right is the single most valuable judgment in the report.</p>
<h4>CVSS 4.0: measuring severity</h4>
<p>The <strong>Common Vulnerability Scoring System</strong> v4.0 (published by FIRST in November 2023; spec document June 2024) produces a 0–10 severity score from four metric groups. The <strong>Base</strong> group is the flaw's intrinsic traits: attack vector (Network, Adjacent, Local, Physical), attack complexity, attack requirements, privileges required, user interaction, and impact on the confidentiality, integrity and availability of both the vulnerable system (VC/VI/VA) and subsequent systems (SC/SI/SA). The <strong>Threat</strong> group adds exploit maturity; the <strong>Environmental</strong> group lets you re-weight for your own security requirements (CR/IR/AR) and modified base metrics; the <strong>Supplemental</strong> group (Safety, Automatable, Recovery, Value Density, Response Effort, Provider Urgency) carries extra context without changing the number. The nomenclature names which groups you used: CVSS-B (base only), CVSS-BT (base+threat), CVSS-BE (base+environmental), CVSS-BTE (all three). Bands: none 0.0, low 0.1–3.9, medium 4.0–6.9, high 7.0–8.9, critical 9.0–10.0.</p>
<p>Worked example. The vector <code>CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:N/VC:H/VI:H/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N</code> — network-reachable, low complexity, no privileges, no user interaction, full impact on the vulnerable system — scores <strong>9.3, critical</strong>. Now add real-world threat and environment. If exploit maturity is only Proof-of-Concept and privileges are required, say <code>AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:L/UI:N/VC:H/VI:H/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N</code>, the base is <strong>8.6</strong>; mark exploit maturity Proof-of-Concept (<code>/E:P</code>) and it drops to <strong>7.3</strong>. The same flaw, scored with more context, moves nearly a full band — which is exactly the point: CVSS is designed to be refined, not read off the base alone. Many advisories still carry CVSS v3.1 scores, so always note which version you are reading.</p>
<h4>The trap: severity is not risk</h4>
<p>CVSS measures how damaging a flaw is if exploited; it does not tell you how <em>likely</em> exploitation is here. Two more signals estimate likelihood. <strong>EPSS</strong> (the Exploit Prediction Scoring System, maintained by a FIRST special-interest group; version 4 released March 2025) gives the probability a vulnerability will be exploited in the wild in the next 30 days — most CVEs score well under 1%. CISA's <strong>Known Exploited Vulnerabilities</strong> (KEV) catalogue lists vulnerabilities with confirmed, observed exploitation; under Binding Operational Directive 22-01 US federal agencies must remediate new entries within two weeks, a good benchmark for everyone. Layer on <strong>business context</strong> — is the asset internet-facing or internal, does it hold regulated data, is there a compensating control — and the queue sorts itself.</p>
<table><tr><th>Finding</th><th>CVSS</th><th>EPSS</th><th>KEV</th><th>Context</th><th>Priority</th></tr>
<tr><td>VPN appliance remote-code flaw</td><td>9.8 (v3.1)</td><td>0.94</td><td>Yes</td><td>Internet-facing</td><td>P1 — mitigate today</td></tr>
<tr><td>Login auth bypass</td><td>8.6 (v4.0)</td><td>0.30</td><td>No</td><td>Internet-facing, customer data</td><td>P1 — days</td></tr>
<tr><td>File-server privilege escalation</td><td>7.8</td><td>0.02</td><td>No</td><td>Internal, critical data</td><td>P2 — 14–30 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Printer firmware</td><td>9.1</td><td>0.001</td><td>No</td><td>Isolated VLAN, no internet</td><td>P3 — 90 days; segmentation compensates</td></tr>
<tr><td>TLS 1.0 on an internal app</td><td>5.3</td><td>low</td><td>No</td><td>Internal</td><td>P4 — next window</td></tr></table>
<p>Read the printer row against the login row: the printer has a higher raw CVSS (9.1 vs 8.6) but a far lower risk, because EPSS is near zero, it is not on the KEV list, and segmentation puts it out of reach — while the login's lower score sits on an internet-facing system holding customer data. Priority follows risk, not the biggest number. That reasoning is what you defend to the client.</p>
<h4>Turning it into a plan</h4>
<p>Write the result as remediation SLAs the client can adopt: P1 (on KEV, or critical and internet-facing) within 72 hours to 7 days; P2 within 14–30 days; P3 within 60–90 days; P4 at the next maintenance window. External anchors you can cite: BOD 22-01's two weeks for KEV, and PCI DSS's one month for critical patches. Anything that cannot meet its SLA gets a documented exception with a named risk owner, a compensating control and an expiry date. This is the same vulnerability-management arithmetic the blue team runs (Domain 21) — you produce the input, they run the loop.</p>
<h4>Why environmental metrics are not optional</h4>
<p>The CVSS 4.0 Environmental group exists precisely so the client's reality changes the number, and ignoring it is the most common scoring error. Consider two identical flaws: one on a system whose confidentiality the business rates critical (customer records) and one on a system whose confidentiality barely matters (a public brochure cache). Setting the confidentiality security requirement high (CR:H) on the first and low on the second pulls their scores apart in the direction that matches reality, so the report's priority order reflects the business rather than a generic average. Likewise, if a compensating control means an attacker needs local access they do not have, the modified attack-vector metric lowers the score honestly. A base score is a vendor's view of a flaw in the abstract; an environmental score is your view of it in <em>this</em> estate, and the client is paying for the second. State the nomenclature (CVSS-BE or CVSS-BTE) so the reader knows you did this and can reproduce it. Done well, the environmental refinement often reconciles the score with the business-context priority you would have assigned by judgment anyway — which is the sign you scored it right.</p>
<h4>On the exam</h4>
<p>PenTest+ and CySA+ both test CVSS interpretation and the severity-versus-risk distinction; expect a vector to read, a band to assign, and a prioritization scenario where the highest CVSS is deliberately not the highest risk. Know the four metric groups, the bands, and that EPSS (likelihood) and KEV (observed exploitation) complement CVSS (severity). The exam wants the judgment, not just the number.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Prioritizing a 20-finding report for a 40-person company.</strong> The scan-and-verify pass yields 20 findings. Sorted by raw CVSS, a 9.1 printer-firmware flaw tops the list. Sorted by risk, it drops to P3: EPSS 0.001, not on KEV, and the printer sits on an isolated VLAN with no internet route — a compensating control. The real P1s are two internet-facing issues: a VPN appliance flaw that is on the KEV catalogue with EPSS 0.94 (mitigate today), and an authentication-bypass on the customer login scoring 8.6 in CVSS 4.0 on an asset holding regulated data. You write SLAs — P1 within 72 hours, P2 within 30 days, P3 within 90 — and a one-page executive summary that says, in plain language, 'two internet-facing problems could expose customer accounts; fix these this week, the rest over the quarter.' The client fixes the two that matter first, not the biggest number, and the retest three weeks later confirms both. That is CVSS, EPSS, KEV and business context doing exactly what severity scores alone cannot.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"The highest CVSS is always the top priority.\" CVSS is severity; risk also needs likelihood (EPSS, KEV) and business context (exposure, data, compensating controls).","\"A CVSS base score is the final answer.\" CVSS 4.0 is designed to be refined with threat and environmental metrics; the base alone can be a full band too high or low.","\"EPSS and KEV are the same thing.\" EPSS predicts probability of exploitation in 30 days; KEV lists vulnerabilities already observed being exploited."],
problems:[
{q:"Distinguish severity from risk in one sentence each.", a:"Severity (CVSS) is how damaging a flaw is if exploited; risk is how likely it is to hurt this specific business and how much (likelihood × impact × exposure)."},
{q:"Name the four CVSS 4.0 metric groups and what each adds.", a:"Base (intrinsic traits and impact), Threat (exploit maturity), Environmental (your security requirements and modified base), Supplemental (extra context that doesn't change the score)."},
{q:"A printer flaw is CVSS 9.1 but a login flaw is 8.6. Why might the login be the higher priority?", a:"Risk, not raw severity: the login is internet-facing with customer data (higher likelihood and impact), while the printer is on an isolated VLAN with near-zero EPSS and not on KEV — segmentation compensates."},
{q:"What do EPSS and the KEV catalogue each tell you?", a:"EPSS: probability of exploitation in the next 30 days (likelihood). KEV: vulnerabilities confirmed to be actively exploited — treat as urgent regardless of score."},
{q:"Convert a prioritization into SLAs with an external anchor.", a:"P1 (KEV or critical+internet-facing) in 72 h–7 days (cite BOD 22-01's 2 weeks for KEV / PCI DSS 1 month for critical); P2 14–30 days; P3 60–90; P4 next window; exceptions documented with owner, compensating control and expiry."},
{q:"What does the CVSS nomenclature CVSS-BTE mean, and why state it?", a:"The score used Base, Threat and Environmental metrics; stating it tells the reader how much context shaped the number so they can trust and reproduce it."},
{q:"Why always note whether a score is CVSS v3.1 or v4.0?", a:"The versions use different metrics and formulas, so the same flaw can score differently; a reader must know which scale to compare against."}
],
checks:[{q:"CVSS measures what?",a:"Severity — how damaging a flaw is if exploited."},{q:"EPSS measures what?",a:"Probability of exploitation in the next 30 days."},{q:"What is the KEV catalogue?",a:"CISA's list of vulnerabilities confirmed actively exploited."}],
explain:"Give ADA a mixed list of findings with CVSS scores and have it rank them; then correct the ranking using EPSS, KEV and business context, explaining each move."},

{id:"p8m7", title:"Assessing Modern Estates: Web, Cloud and Active Directory as Configuration and Design Review", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Frame cloud, directory and web assessment as configuration and design review.","Explain the shared-responsibility model and the provider rules that bound cloud testing.","Explain the common Active Directory and cloud misconfigurations by design flaw and fix.","Map each weakness to a defensive control and a detection."],
body:`<p>The question this module answers: how do you assess a modern environment — a web estate, a cloud account, an Active Directory domain — when "hacking in" is the wrong frame and the real work is reviewing configuration and design? Most serious findings in these estates are not exotic exploits; they are <em>misconfigurations and design decisions</em> that an authorized reviewer can read directly, explain, and hand back as fixes. This is where offensive and defensive skill merge completely.</p>
<h4>Cloud: shared responsibility and provider rules</h4>
<p>In the cloud the provider secures the infrastructure and you secure what you put on it — the <strong>shared-responsibility model</strong> (Domain 17). Assessment is therefore mostly a review of the customer's configuration: identity and access management policies, storage permissions, network security groups, logging, and key management. The provider's rules bound what you may do: AWS, for example, lets customers test a defined list of their own services without prior approval but forbids denial-of-service (even simulated), DNS zone walking, and testing the provider's own infrastructure; red/blue/purple exercises and stress tests need a separate request. You assess <em>your client's</em> cloud resources, never the provider's, and always within those terms — which are themselves a form of scope (Module 1). The classic findings are configuration findings: a storage bucket readable by the public, an over-privileged role that grants far more than its job needs, access keys with no rotation, or disabled audit logging. Each is reported as a design/least-privilege failure with a concrete fix and the log source that would detect abuse.</p>
<h4>Active Directory: a design review, defensively</h4>
<p>Active Directory (Domain 16) is where most enterprise internal assessments live, and modern practice treats the AD portion as an assumed-breach <em>design review</em> — OSCP+ even hands you a starting credential. The point is never a trophy; it is to show the client where privilege is structured badly so they can fix the structure. The recurring design weaknesses, stated as weaknesses and fixes:</p>
<table><tr><th>Design weakness</th><th>Why it's risky</th><th>Remediation / defense</th></tr>
<tr><td>Flat privilege (many admins, no tiering)</td><td>One compromised workstation reaches domain admin</td><td>Tiered administration (Domain 16), separate admin accounts, PAW workstations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Over-privileged service accounts</td><td>A shared service identity becomes a master key</td><td>Least privilege, group-managed service accounts, scoped delegation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legacy cryptography enabled (e.g. RC4 in Kerberos)</td><td>Weak algorithms undermine authentication</td><td>Disable legacy ciphers; Microsoft is phasing RC4 out of Kerberos through 2026 updates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weak or reused privileged passwords</td><td>Credential reuse spreads a single breach</td><td>Long unique passwords, LAPS for local admins, MFA for admins</td></tr>
<tr><td>Excessive trust and stale objects</td><td>Forgotten accounts and broad trusts widen the blast radius</td><td>Regular access reviews, remove stale objects, constrain trusts</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Every row is a configuration or design fact a reviewer can observe and a defender can change; none requires teaching an attack. The deliverable is a prioritized plan to restructure privilege, retire legacy protocols and add monitoring — mapped, in Module 8, to the detections that would catch abuse.</p>
<h4>Web estates: design review plus the WSTG</h4>
<p>A web assessment is Module 5's vulnerability classes applied systematically with the WSTG checklist and verified against ASVS — but framed as design review wherever possible: is authorization enforced server-side on every request; are secrets stored and transmitted properly (Domain 25 cryptography); is input treated as data at every trust boundary; are errors and logging handled so failures are safe and visible (A09, A10 of the 2025 Top 10). The most durable findings are architectural: a missing authorization layer, a trust placed in the client, a secret in the wrong place. You report the design fix, not a patch for one endpoint.</p>
<h4>The through-line</h4>
<p>Across cloud, directory and web, the professional's contribution is the same: read the configuration and the design, find where trust and privilege are misplaced, prove it safely and minimally, and hand back a structural fix and a detection. This is why Domains 16 (Windows/AD), 17 (cloud) and 5 (web) are prerequisites in spirit — you cannot review a design you do not understand, and the fix is always expressed in the defender's language.</p>
<h4>Identity is the new perimeter</h4>
<p>One idea unifies all three estates: in a modern environment the firewall is no longer the boundary that matters — <strong>identity</strong> is. A cloud role, an AD service account and a web session are all identities, and nearly every serious finding is a case of an identity holding more trust than it should or proving itself too weakly. That is why the recurring remediations rhyme across estates: least privilege (give each identity only what its job needs), strong authentication (MFA, retire weak protocols), and monitoring of identity use (who assumed which role, which account logged in from where). An assessor who learns to see cloud, directory and web as one identity fabric writes findings a client can act on coherently — tighten these roles, retire these legacy credentials, watch these privileged accounts — rather than a scattered list. This is also the bridge to zero-trust architecture (Domain 7): assume breach, verify every request, and never trust an identity by its network location alone.</p>
<h4>Proving reach without causing harm</h4>
<p>Showing a misconfiguration is real sometimes means demonstrating that one weak identity can reach another resource, which is exactly where minimal-impact discipline (Module 2) governs. You prove the <em>reach</em> — that the over-privileged role can read the sensitive bucket, that the flat AD lets a help-desk account touch an administrative resource — to the least extent that establishes the finding, with evidence and timestamps, and you stop there. You do not pivot further for drama, you do not copy the data, and you note in the report that the demonstration was deliberately limited. The client needs to believe the risk and fix the structure; they do not need you to have exercised the whole attack path to do so.</p>
<h4>On the exam</h4>
<p>PenTest+ PT0-003 added weight on cloud and on post-exploitation and lateral movement, and its scenarios lean on configuration review; OSCP+ builds the whole Active Directory set around an assumed-breach design scenario. Expect to identify an over-privileged role, a public storage bucket, a flat-privilege AD, or a legacy-protocol weakness and to state the least-privilege or hardening fix. The exam rewards the reviewer's eye, not a break-in.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A cloud and directory review for a mid-size client.</strong> In the cloud account, authorized to test only the client's own listed services and forbidden from any denial-of-service, you review configuration and find three design findings: a storage bucket set to public-read that holds exported reports (fix: make it private, add a bucket policy, turn on access logging); an application role granted full administrative permissions when it needs read access to one service (fix: scope the policy to least privilege); and audit logging disabled in two regions (fix: enable it everywhere, because without logs the blue team is blind). In the Active Directory review, starting from a standard user as agreed, you observe flat privilege — ordinary help-desk staff hold rights that reach domain administration — and a service account with far more access than its job needs (fix: tiered administration, scoped service accounts, MFA for admins, and retire RC4 in line with Microsoft's 2026 Kerberos hardening). None of this needed an exploit demonstration; it needed a reviewer who understands cloud IAM and AD design well enough to read the misconfiguration, prove the reach minimally, and write the structural fix. The report's remediation plan restructures privilege and turns logging on — and Module 8's detections make sure the next attempt is seen.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Cloud and AD testing means breaking in.\" It is mostly configuration and design review — reading misplaced trust and privilege — and reporting structural fixes.","\"You can pentest anything in your cloud account freely.\" Provider rules bound it: you test only your own listed resources, never the provider's infrastructure, and DoS (even simulated) is forbidden without separate approval.","\"AD findings are about clever attacks.\" They are about design: flat privilege, over-privileged service accounts and legacy protocols — fixed by tiering, least privilege and retiring old ciphers."],
problems:[
{q:"Why is cloud assessment mostly a configuration review, and what model explains it?", a:"The shared-responsibility model: the provider secures the infrastructure, the customer secures their configuration — so the findings are IAM, storage, network and logging misconfigurations in the customer's setup."},
{q:"Name three bounds a cloud provider's testing policy typically places on you.", a:"Test only your own listed resources (not the provider's infrastructure); no denial-of-service even simulated; some activities (C2, stress tests, red/blue/purple) need separate approval."},
{q:"State the risk and the fix for flat privilege in Active Directory.", a:"Risk: one compromised workstation can reach domain admin. Fix: tiered administration, separate admin accounts and privileged-access workstations."},
{q:"Why are over-privileged service accounts a recurring finding, and how is it remediated?", a:"A shared service identity with excess rights becomes a master key if compromised; remediate with least privilege, group-managed service accounts and scoped delegation."},
{q:"A storage bucket holding reports is world-readable. How do you report it?", a:"As a least-privilege/misconfiguration finding: make it private with a restrictive policy, enable access logging, and note the business impact (data exposure) and the log source that detects abuse."},
{q:"Why frame the strongest web findings as design, not patches?", a:"Architectural flaws — missing server-side authorization, trust in the client, misplaced secrets — recur across endpoints; the structural fix closes the class, a per-endpoint patch does not."},
{q:"What is the common through-line across cloud, AD and web assessment?", a:"Read configuration and design, find misplaced trust and privilege, prove it safely and minimally, and hand back a structural fix plus a detection — in the defender's language."}
],
checks:[{q:"What model governs cloud security duties?",a:"The shared-responsibility model."},{q:"The fix for flat AD privilege?",a:"Tiered administration and least privilege."},{q:"How is cloud/AD assessment best framed?",a:"As configuration and design review."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA how you'd review a client's cloud account and AD domain as a design review — what you'd look for, why provider rules bound you, and how you'd write each finding as a structural fix."},

{id:"p8m8", title:"Working With Defenders: Purple Teaming, Detection Validation and MITRE ATT&CK", minutes:50,
outcomes:["Explain red, blue and purple teaming and when each adds value.","Use MITRE ATT&CK as the shared language between offense and defense.","Explain detection validation and how a finding becomes a detection.","Tie evidence, reporting and retesting to measurable defensive improvement."],
body:`<p>The question this module answers: once you can find weaknesses, how do you make an organization measurably harder to attack — not just hand them a list? The mature answer is to work <em>with</em> the defenders, not against them, and to measure success in detections built and gaps closed. This is the frame the whole domain has been building toward: the offensive skill exists to improve the defense.</p>
<h4>Red, blue and purple</h4>
<p>A <strong>red team</strong> emulates a realistic adversary against a mature organization, often stealthily, to test whether the defenders detect and respond — it answers "would we catch a real attacker?" A <strong>blue team</strong> is the defenders: the SOC, incident response and engineering (Domain 21). A <strong>purple team</strong> is the two working together in the open: the offensive side runs a technique under authorization while the defensive side watches their telemetry, and together they confirm whether it was logged, alerted and could be responded to. Purple teaming is usually the highest-value engagement for a growing organization, because every exercise ends with a concrete improvement — a new detection, a tuned alert, a closed gap — rather than only a verdict.</p>
<h4>MITRE ATT&CK: the shared language</h4>
<p><strong>MITRE ATT&CK</strong> is a free, continuously updated knowledge base of adversary <em>tactics</em> (the goal — initial access, persistence, privilege escalation, lateral movement, exfiltration) and <em>techniques</em> (how, with stable IDs like T-numbers), across Enterprise, Mobile and ICS matrices. It is the lingua franca that lets an assessor say "we exercised this technique" and a defender say "here is our detection for it" and both mean the same thing. The framework evolves — version 19 (April 2026) split the old Defense Evasion tactic into Stealth and Defense Impairment and added techniques for AI misuse; version 20 (October 2026) added more — so cite the version. ATT&CK is also how you map coverage: lay your findings and the client's detections over the matrix and the white space is the gap to close.</p>
<h4>Detection validation: turning a finding into a detection</h4>
<p>This is the practical heart of purple teaming. For each technique in scope you ask: when we did this (under authorization, in the open), did the right telemetry exist (the logs and EDR events from Domains 7 and 21); did it reach the SIEM; did an alert fire; was it the right severity; could the on-call analyst act on it? Wherever the answer is no, the outcome is a new or tuned <strong>detection</strong> expressed as a rule (a Sigma rule, an EDR query) mapped to its ATT&CK technique. A finding thereby becomes a durable capability: next time, the real attacker is seen. That is a far better deliverable than "we got in," and it is why detection validation is the fastest-maturing part of the profession.</p>
<h4>Evidence, reporting and measurable improvement</h4>
<p>Everything from Module 4 applies, sharpened: your evidence (timestamps, the exact actions taken) lets the blue team line up your activity against their logs to the second, which is how they confirm whether a detection fired. The report adds a coverage view — techniques exercised, detected, missed — and the remediation includes both the fix (close the weakness) and the detection (catch the attempt). The <strong>retest</strong> (Module 4) now has a second dimension: not only "is the vulnerability fixed?" but "does the detection fire now?" Measurable improvement — mean time to detect, percentage of techniques covered, gaps closed since last engagement — is what turns a one-off test into a security program (Domains 7, 21, 33).</p>
<h4>Why detection beats prevention alone</h4>
<p>A hard truth underlies the whole purple-team discipline: prevention eventually fails. Patches lag, a new flaw appears, a user is fooled, a configuration drifts. An organization that invested only in prevention is blind the day prevention fails; one that invested in detection sees the intruder and can respond before the damage spreads. That is why converting a finding into a detection is often more durable than the fix itself — the specific vulnerability may be patched next month, but the detection for the <em>technique</em> catches the next attacker who tries the same move through a different flaw. Mapping detections to MITRE ATT&CK techniques rather than to individual vulnerabilities is what makes them durable: techniques change far more slowly than the thousands of CVEs that use them. This is the assessor's highest-leverage contribution, and it is why the industry's fastest-growing roles sit at the offense-defense boundary.</p>
<h4>Evidence handling in a collaborative engagement</h4>
<p>Working in the open with the defenders does not relax evidence discipline — it raises it. Because the SOC is correlating against your actions in real time, your log of exactly what you did and when must be precise to the second, and it must be shared in a controlled channel, because it is effectively a map of how to exercise the client's weaknesses. You still redact sensitive data in the written deliverable, still store raw evidence encrypted, and still delete it per the contract at close. The difference is that the evidence now serves a second purpose beyond proof and legal protection: it is the ground truth the blue team uses to decide whether each detection it built actually works. Good evidence is therefore not just defensible — it is the raw material of the client's new visibility.</p>
<h4>The whole domain in one frame</h4>
<p>Authorization (Module 1) makes the work legal; methodology and standards (Module 2) make it repeatable; the toolkit and labs (Module 3) build the skill lawfully; threat modelling and the vulnerability classes (Module 5) let you find and explain weaknesses by root cause; risk scoring (Module 6) prioritizes them honestly; configuration and design review (Module 7) is where most real findings live; and working with defenders (this module) converts all of it into detections and fixes. The report and retest (Module 4) are the product throughout. Offense exists to serve defense — that is the profession.</p>
<h4>On the exam</h4>
<p>ATT&CK appears across PenTest+, CEH and the blue-team certs as the mapping language; expect to match a described behavior to a tactic, or to pick purple teaming as the right engagement when the goal is to improve detection. The deeper point the exams and employers reward is the mindset: the measure of a great assessor is not what they broke, but how much harder the organization is to attack when they leave.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A purple-team exercise that leaves the client stronger.</strong> Over a week, the offensive side and the client's SOC sit in the same channel. For each technique in scope — chosen from the MITRE ATT&CK matrix to match the threats that actually target this industry — the offensive side performs it under authorization and announces the time; the SOC immediately checks whether the telemetry existed, reached the SIEM, and raised an alert. Early on, a credential-access technique produces nothing in the SIEM: the logs existed on the host but were never forwarded. That becomes finding one, with a fix (forward the logs) and a new Sigma detection mapped to its ATT&CK technique. A lateral-movement technique fires an alert but at low severity buried under noise: finding two is a tuning change. By week's end the report shows a coverage map — techniques exercised, detected, and missed — plus six new or tuned detections. The retest a month later re-runs the same techniques and confirms five now alert correctly. The client did not just learn they had gaps; they left the engagement able to see attacks they were blind to before. That is the whole point of the domain, delivered.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Red teaming is just a louder pentest.\" A red team emulates a realistic adversary to test detection and response; a pentest enumerates and verifies weaknesses — different goals.","\"Purple teaming is a compromise between red and blue.\" It is offense and defense working in the open together so every technique ends in a validated detection or a closed gap.","\"ATT&CK is for defenders only.\" It is the shared language both sides use to describe techniques and map coverage; cite the version, since it changes."],
problems:[
{q:"Distinguish red, blue and purple teaming by goal.", a:"Red: emulate a realistic adversary to test whether defenders detect and respond. Blue: the defenders (SOC, IR, engineering). Purple: both working in the open so each technique ends in a validated detection or closed gap."},
{q:"What does MITRE ATT&CK provide, and why cite the version?", a:"A shared knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques (with stable IDs) across Enterprise/Mobile/ICS; it changes over time (v19 split Defense Evasion; v20 added techniques), so the version pins what you mean."},
{q:"Walk the questions of detection validation for one technique.", a:"When performed under authorization: did the telemetry exist; did it reach the SIEM; did an alert fire; at the right severity; could the analyst act? Any 'no' becomes a new/tuned detection mapped to its ATT&CK technique."},
{q:"How does a finding become a durable defensive capability?", a:"It is turned into a detection rule (e.g. Sigma or an EDR query) mapped to its ATT&CK technique, so the next attempt is seen — a capability, not just a line in a report."},
{q:"How does retesting differ in a purple-team context?", a:"It gains a second dimension: not only 'is the vulnerability fixed?' but 'does the detection fire now?' — verifying both the fix and the new visibility."},
{q:"Why is purple teaming often the highest-value engagement for a growing organization?", a:"Every exercise ends with a concrete improvement — a new detection, a tuned alert, a closed gap — rather than only a pass/fail verdict."},
{q:"State the one-sentence frame that ties the whole domain together.", a:"Offense exists to serve defense: authorization makes it legal, methodology makes it repeatable, and the measure of success is how much harder the organization is to attack when you leave."}
],
checks:[{q:"What is purple teaming?",a:"Offense and defense working together in the open to validate and build detections."},{q:"What is MITRE ATT&CK?",a:"A shared knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques."},{q:"The goal of detection validation?",a:"Confirm an attack technique is logged, alerted and actionable — and build the detection where it isn't."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA how you'd run a purple-team week with a client's SOC using MITRE ATT&CK, and how each finding turns into a detection measured at the retest."}
],
listening:[],
labs:[
{id:"p8lab1",title:"Build a home pentest lab",goal:"Stand up a legal practice range on your own machine.",steps:["Install VirtualBox; create an attacker VM (Kali) and a target VM (OWASP Juice Shop or Metasploitable) on a host-only network so nothing leaks to the internet.","Confirm the two VMs can reach each other and nothing else.","Document the network so you can prove isolation.","Explain to ADA why isolation is both safe and legally essential."],deliverable:"A diagram of your isolated lab network."},
{id:"p8lab2",title:"PortSwigger Web Security Academy",goal:"Learn web vulnerabilities in the vendor's free, legal labs.",steps:["Create a free account at the Web Security Academy.","Complete the introductory SQL injection and access-control learning paths — all in their sandbox.","For each vulnerability class, summarize for ADA: what it is, how it's found, and the Domain 7 fix.","Note how each maps to the OWASP Top 10."],deliverable:"Your written vulnerability summaries with fixes."},
{id:"p8lab3",title:"Capture the flag, with a report",goal:"Apply the methodology and practice the deliverable.",steps:["On TryHackMe, complete a beginner room end to end.","Keep notes in the five-phase structure (recon → scan → access → post → report).","Write a one-page client-style report: findings, risk ratings, evidence, and remediation.","Have ADA review the report for clarity and prioritization."],deliverable:"Your CTF report in professional format."},
{id:"p8lab4",title:"Scope an engagement",goal:"Practice the pre-engagement skill that carries the most value.",steps:["Given a fictional client (ADA will role-play), draft a scope and rules of engagement: targets in/out, allowed methods, test window, emergency contact.","Decide black/white/grey box and justify it.","Identify what's explicitly forbidden (e.g. DoS, social engineering) and why.","Have ADA, as the client, poke holes in your scope."],deliverable:"Your scope + RoE document."}
],
capstone:{title:"Run a mock engagement end to end", brief:"On a sanctioned platform (TryHackMe/HTB) or your own isolated lab, run a complete mock engagement: a scoped plan and RoE anchored to PTES and NIST SP 800-115, a threat model, the phases documented, findings scored with CVSS 4.0 and prioritized by business risk (EPSS/KEV/context), and a full professional report (executive summary + technical findings with root cause, risk ratings, evidence, impact and remediation), plus a retest plan and at least two detections mapped to MITRE ATT&CK. Present it to ADA as the client's security lead, defending your prioritization, your detections and your ethics throughout.", rubric:["Explicit scope and rules of engagement written before any testing, anchored to a named standard; all work stays within them.","The phases are documented with minimal-impact discipline, a threat model and reproducible evidence.","Findings carry root cause, CVSS 4.0 scores and remediation, and are prioritized by business risk (EPSS/KEV/context), not raw severity.","The report has an executive summary and technical findings; a retest plan and at least two ATT&CK-mapped detections turn findings into defensive capability.","Ethical and legal boundaries are respected and defended throughout."]},
exam:[
{id:"p8q1",type:"mc",q:"What single thing makes a penetration test legal rather than a crime?",options:["Using professional tools","Explicit written authorization within an agreed scope","Causing no damage","Having a certification"],answer:1,why:"Authorization and scope are the entire legal basis; the CFAA criminalizes unauthorized access regardless of intent or damage."},
{id:"p8q2",type:"mc",q:"Which document defines the allowed techniques, timing and limits of a test?",options:["The NDA","The Rules of Engagement","The invoice","The CVE list"],answer:1,why:"The RoE sets methods, windows, rate limits, and the emergency contact."},
{id:"p8q3",type:"mc",q:"Put the engagement phases in order:",options:["Exploit, recon, scan, report, post","Recon, scanning/enumeration, gaining access, post-exploitation, reporting","Scan, report, recon, exploit, post","Reporting, recon, scan, exploit, post"],answer:1,why:"Recon → scan/enumerate → gain access → post-exploitation → report."},
{id:"p8q4",type:"mc",q:"Black-box testing means the tester:",options:["Has full system information","Has no prior information (simulates an outsider)","Is an employee","Only tests physically"],answer:1,why:"Black box = no prior knowledge; white box = full info; grey box = partial."},
{id:"p8q5",type:"mc",q:"The real deliverable of a penetration test is:",options:["Proof of a break-in","A clear, prioritized report with remediation","A copy of the data","A list of tools used"],answer:1,why:"Clients pay for actionable findings and fixes, not the intrusion itself."},
{id:"p8q6",type:"mc",q:"Where should you build hands-on skills?",options:["Any website you find","Sanctioned labs (TryHackMe/HTB/PortSwigger) and your own isolated VMs","A friend's company","Public Wi-Fi"],answer:1,why:"Only authorized, purpose-built environments and systems you own are legal."},
{id:"p8q7",type:"mc",q:"You discover out-of-scope data mid-test. You should:",options:["Keep going to be thorough","Stop, document, and notify your point of contact","Delete it","Post about it"],answer:1,why:"Out-of-scope findings are escalated, never acted on."},
{id:"p8q8",type:"mc",q:"Which certification is the respected hands-on practical milestone?",options:["CEH","Security+","OSCP","ITF+"],answer:2,why:"OSCP's practical exam proves you can do the work; Security+ is the earlier foundation."},
{id:"p8q9",type:"mc",q:"Why does the defensive domain (Domain 7) come before this one?",options:["It's easier","Every offensive phase mirrors a defensive control, and the output is fixes","It's unrelated","To fill time"],answer:1,why:"You can't produce useful remediation or avoid harm without defensive mastery."},
{id:"p8q10",type:"mc",q:"'Minimal impact' during a test means:",options:["Test as aggressively as possible","Prove findings without exploiting further than needed or destroying data","Skip the risky systems","Only scan, never verify"],answer:1,why:"Controlled, documented, reversible assessment — prove impact without causing harm."},
{id:"p8q11",type:"sa",q:"Explain why authorization, not skill or intent, is what separates a penetration tester from a criminal, and how a professional operates within that boundary.",answer:"The same technical action is a paid service with signed authorization and a felony without it — the CFAA criminalizes unauthorized access regardless of good intent or lack of damage. A professional therefore operates only inside a written scope and rules of engagement, carries an authorization letter, practices solely on sanctioned platforms and systems they own, stops and escalates when they hit out-of-scope data or risk of harm, and discloses findings responsibly. The clean ethical record and judgment are the pentester's most valuable assets.",rubric:"States authorization/scope (not skill/intent) as the legal line, cites unauthorized access being criminal regardless of damage, and describes operating within written scope + sanctioned practice + escalation/disclosure."},
{id:"p8q12",type:"sa",q:"Describe the five phases of an engagement and give the Domain 7 defensive counter for each offensive phase.",answer:"Reconnaissance (map the attack surface) — countered by reducing public exposure/attack surface. Scanning & enumeration (find services, versions, weaknesses) — countered by patching, hardening, closing unneeded services. Gaining access/exploitation (safely prove a weakness is real) — countered by secure coding, input validation, patching. Post-exploitation (assess reach and business impact without harm) — countered by least privilege, segmentation, monitoring. Reporting (document findings, risk, remediation) — the deliverable, which feeds all the above defenses. Every offensive step mirrors a defensive control, which is why defense is prerequisite.",rubric:"Lists the five phases in order with a correct Domain 7 defensive counter for each of the four offensive phases, and notes reporting as the deliverable."},
{id:"p8q13",type:"sa",q:"Why is the report the true product of a penetration test, and what makes a finding well-written and well-prioritized?",answer:"Clients pay to understand and fix their weaknesses, not to be broken into; a dramatic intrusion with a poor report fails, while a modest finding with an excellent report succeeds. A well-written finding is self-contained: a clear title/description with root cause, a risk rating (e.g. CVSS) combining severity and likelihood, reproducible evidence, the business impact, and concrete prioritized remediation. Findings are ordered by business risk (likelihood × impact and exposure), not raw severity alone — so an internet-facing flaw handling customer data may outrank a higher-CVSS internal one — so the client fixes what matters most first.",rubric:"Explains the report is the deliverable (fixes, not the break-in), lists finding elements including remediation and risk rating, and explains prioritization by business risk/exposure over raw severity."},
{id:"p8q14",type:"mc",q:"Which standard provides a free rules-of-engagement template and a four-stage methodology (planning, discovery, attack, reporting)?",options:["PTES","NIST SP 800-115","OWASP ASVS","MITRE ATT&CK"],answer:1,why:"NIST SP 800-115 frames planning/discovery/attack/reporting and includes an RoE template in Appendix B; PTES has seven sections, ASVS lists requirements, ATT&CK catalogues techniques."},
{id:"p8q15",type:"mc",q:"A finding is CVSS 9.1 but sits on an isolated internal VLAN with EPSS 0.001 and is not on the KEV catalogue; another is CVSS 8.6 on an internet-facing customer-login system. Which is the higher priority and why?",options:["The 9.1 — always fix the highest CVSS first","The 8.6 — risk considers likelihood and exposure, not just severity","Neither — CVSS is the only input","The 9.1 — internal systems matter more"],answer:1,why:"CVSS is severity; risk also needs likelihood (EPSS/KEV) and business context (exposure, data), so the exposed, reachable login outranks the isolated higher-scored flaw."},
{id:"p8q16",type:"mc",q:"What is the root cause shared by injection, cross-site scripting, SSRF and path traversal?",options:["Weak passwords","Untrusted input crossing a trust boundary and treated as privileged","Missing encryption","Outdated hardware"],answer:1,why:"Each treats attacker-controlled input as code, a command, a URL or a filename rather than inert data; keeping input as data on the trusted side prevents all of them."},
{id:"p8q17",type:"mc",q:"In the cloud shared-responsibility model, an authorized assessment mainly consists of:",options:["Testing the provider's data centers","Reviewing the customer's configuration (IAM, storage, network, logging)","Running denial-of-service against the region","Decrypting the provider's keys"],answer:1,why:"The provider secures the infrastructure; the customer secures their configuration, so assessment reviews IAM/storage/network/logging — and DoS and testing the provider are forbidden."},
{id:"p8q18",type:"mc",q:"What best describes a purple-team engagement?",options:["A stealthier red team","Offense and defense working in the open so each technique ends in a validated detection or closed gap","A compliance audit","A vulnerability scan"],answer:1,why:"Purple teaming pairs the offensive side with the SOC to confirm detection and build it where missing, mapped to MITRE ATT&CK."},
{id:"p8q19",type:"sa",q:"A client has a tight budget. Using scope, severity/risk and reporting together, explain how you decide what goes in the report's top-priority section and how you justify leaving a high-CVSS finding lower.",answer:"First, everything must be inside the agreed scope — out-of-scope findings are escalated, not ranked. Within scope, you separate severity (CVSS 4.0) from risk: you temper each CVSS score with likelihood (EPSS, and whether it is on CISA's KEV catalogue) and business context (internet-facing vs isolated, regulated data, compensating controls such as segmentation). The top-priority section holds the highest-risk findings — typically internet-facing, exploited-in-the-wild or KEV-listed issues on assets that hold sensitive data — even if their raw CVSS is lower. A high-CVSS finding can sit lower when EPSS is near zero, it is not on KEV, and a compensating control (e.g. an isolated VLAN) makes it unreachable; you document that reasoning and an exception with a risk owner and expiry. The report states each finding's root cause, evidence, business impact and remediation, with SLAs, so the client spends a limited budget where it reduces the most real risk and can defend the choice.",rubric:"Ties scope (only in-scope findings ranked), severity vs risk (CVSS tempered by EPSS/KEV/business context), and reporting (prioritized top section, documented exception for a lower-ranked high-CVSS item, remediation + SLAs) into one defensible decision."},
{id:"p8q20",type:"sa",q:"Describe, end to end, how a single authorized finding becomes both a fix and a durable defensive capability, naming the standards and the retest.",answer:"Threat modelling (PTES/NIST SP 800-115) directs you to a high-value target; you verify a weakness with minimal impact and reproducible evidence. In the report the finding states its root cause, a CVSS 4.0 score tempered by EPSS/KEV/business context, the business impact, and a class-closing remediation (the fix). Working with defenders (purple teaming), you validate detection: when the technique is performed in the open, did telemetry exist, reach the SIEM and alert? Where it did not, you create a detection rule mapped to its MITRE ATT&CK technique — the durable capability. At the retest you confirm two things: the vulnerability is genuinely fixed and the detection now fires. Measured over time (gaps closed, mean time to detect), this turns one finding into both a closed weakness and lasting visibility — offense serving defense.",rubric:"Traces finding → report (root cause, CVSS+context, remediation) → detection validation mapped to ATT&CK (purple teaming) → retest verifying both fix and detection, naming PTES/NIST, CVSS/EPSS/KEV and ATT&CK, and framing offense as serving defense."}
],
cards:[
{id:"p8c1",front:"authorization",back:"Explicit written permission from a system owner to test named targets within scope; the one thing that makes hacking legal (CFAA defense)."},
{id:"p8c2",front:"CFAA",back:"US Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (18 U.S.C. 1030): unauthorized access is a federal crime; authorization is the entire defense (intent/damage irrelevant)."},
{id:"p8c3",front:"scope",back:"Exactly which systems, apps, ranges and accounts are in-bounds and which are explicitly out; third parties are usually out and separately protected."},
{id:"p8c4",front:"rules of engagement",back:"Agreed methods, timing, rate limits, data handling, stop conditions and emergency contact; NIST SP 800-115 Appendix B is a template."},
{id:"p8c5",front:"authorization letter & NDA",back:"Signed permission from a system owner (carried during the test) plus an agreement to protect the secrets you see."},
{id:"p8c6",front:"Van Buren",back:"2021 Supreme Court case narrowing CFAA 'exceeds authorized access' to reaching off-limits areas, not misusing access you legitimately hold."},
{id:"p8c7",front:"safe harbour",back:"A written promise in a bug-bounty policy not to pursue legal action for good-faith testing that stays within the published scope (see disclose.io)."},
{id:"p8c8",front:"responsible disclosure",back:"Report flaws privately to the owner, allow time to fix, never sell or publicize; coordinated disclosure is the industry norm."},
{id:"p8c9",front:"PTES",back:"Penetration Testing Execution Standard — seven sections: pre-engagement, intelligence gathering, threat modelling, vulnerability analysis, exploitation, post-exploitation, reporting."},
{id:"p8c10",front:"NIST SP 800-115",back:"Free US-government methodology: planning, discovery, attack, reporting; includes a rules-of-engagement template."},
{id:"p8c11",front:"five phases",back:"Reconnaissance → scanning/enumeration → gaining access → post-exploitation → reporting."},
{id:"p8c12",front:"black / white / grey box",back:"No prior info (outsider) / full info (thorough) / partial info (like an authenticated user)."},
{id:"p8c13",front:"threat modelling",back:"Identifying what matters, what can go wrong (e.g. STRIDE), and which tests exercise those risks — directing limited hours to where impact lives."},
{id:"p8c14",front:"offense mirrors defense",back:"Each offensive phase has a Domain 7 counter (recon↔reduce surface, scan↔patch/harden, exploit↔secure code, post↔least-privilege/segmentation)."},
{id:"p8c15",front:"minimal impact",back:"Prove findings without exploiting further than needed, never destroy data, stay in scope, document evidence, pause if production is at risk."},
{id:"p8c16",front:"toolkit categories",back:"Kali/Parrot distro; OSINT; scanners (nmap, Nessus); web proxy (Burp/ZAP); exploitation framework (Metasploit); all dual-use security tools."},
{id:"p8c17",front:"legal practice grounds",back:"TryHackMe, Hack The Box, PortSwigger Web Security Academy, picoCTF, and your own isolated VMs (Juice Shop/DVWA/VulnHub)."},
{id:"p8c18",front:"fundamentals first",back:"Tools surface candidates; Domains 2/3/5/7 let you interpret output, reject false positives and judge impact. A pro reads what a beginner drowns in."},
{id:"p8c19",front:"trust boundary",back:"The line between attacker-controlled input and what the system trusts; most app flaws are untrusted input crossing it and treated as privileged."},
{id:"p8c20",front:"OWASP Top 10",back:"The shared list of top web risks; 2025 edition is led by A01 Broken Access Control and A02 Security Misconfiguration."},
{id:"p8c21",front:"WSTG & ASVS",back:"OWASP's Web Security Testing Guide (what to test) and Application Security Verification Standard 5.0 (requirements to verify, levels L1–L3)."},
{id:"p8c22",front:"CVSS 4.0",back:"FIRST's 0–10 severity score from four metric groups (Base, Threat, Environmental, Supplemental); measures severity, not risk."},
{id:"p8c23",front:"EPSS",back:"Exploit Prediction Scoring System: probability a vulnerability is exploited in the wild within 30 days (likelihood, complements CVSS)."},
{id:"p8c24",front:"KEV catalogue",back:"CISA's list of vulnerabilities confirmed actively exploited; BOD 22-01 requires federal remediation within two weeks."},
{id:"p8c25",front:"severity vs risk",back:"Severity (CVSS) is how bad a flaw is; risk adds likelihood (EPSS/KEV) and business context (exposure, data) — prioritize by risk."},
{id:"p8c26",front:"shared responsibility",back:"In the cloud the provider secures the infrastructure and the customer secures their configuration; assessment is mostly config review."},
{id:"p8c27",front:"configuration & design review",back:"How cloud/AD/web are assessed: read where trust and privilege are misplaced, prove it minimally, hand back a structural fix and a detection."},
{id:"p8c28",front:"least privilege",back:"Grant only the access a role needs; the fix for over-privileged cloud roles and AD service accounts, limiting blast radius."},
{id:"p8c29",front:"MITRE ATT&CK",back:"Free knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques (Enterprise/Mobile/ICS); the shared language between offense and defense."},
{id:"p8c30",front:"purple teaming",back:"Offense and defense working in the open so each technique ends in a validated detection or a closed gap."},
{id:"p8c31",front:"detection validation",back:"Checking that an authorized technique is logged, reaches the SIEM and alerts actionably — and building the detection where it isn't."},
{id:"p8c32",front:"retest",back:"Re-checking after fixes to verify the weakness is genuinely gone (and, in purple teaming, that the new detection fires) — closing the loop."},
{id:"p8c33",front:"the report = the product",back:"Clients pay for clear, prioritized, actionable findings with root cause and remediation, not the break-in itself."},
{id:"p8c34",front:"cert ladder",back:"Security+ (foundation) → eJPT/PenTest+ (entry offensive) → CEH (HR breadth) → OSCP+/GPEN (hands-on milestone)."}
]
});
